WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:07.080
User account artifacts help analysts understand who could access a Mac and what activity may be associated with a profile.

00:00:07.300 --> 00:00:20.340
Local users have home directories under /Users, but enterprise environments may also involve directory services, mobile accounts, single sign-on tools, device management, cloud identity, and shared administrative accounts.

00:00:20.560 --> 00:00:25.200
Account evidence should be interpreted alongside identity and endpoint records when available.

00:00:25.420 --> 00:00:37.020
Home directories can contain documents, downloads, desktop files, browser data, application data, shell history, messaging artifacts, recent item traces, and user-specific configuration.

00:00:37.240 --> 00:00:45.560
Downloads are often especially useful because they may show files obtained from browsers, email, collaboration tools, or other external sources.

00:00:45.780 --> 00:00:51.300
Browser data can help explain web activity, downloads, authentication, and application use.

00:00:51.520 --> 00:00:58.400
Finder activity, recent items, saved state, and application usage traces can support user activity reconstruction.

00:00:58.620 --> 00:01:04.940
They may show files or folders that were opened, applications that were used, or resources that appeared in a user's workflow.

00:01:05.160 --> 00:01:11.480
These artifacts can be very helpful, but they do not automatically prove intent or physical presence at the keyboard.

00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:13.780
User attribution has limits.

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:24.160
A profile artifact may show activity under an account, but the account could have been shared, remotely accessed, compromised, automated, or synced from another service.

00:01:24.380 --> 00:01:32.380
Analysts should compare local artifacts with login records, file metadata, application logs, endpoint telemetry, and business context.

00:01:32.600 --> 00:01:38.040
The question is not only what artifact exists, but whether multiple sources support the same story.
